## 3. Why This Design?

Zig’s embedded approach flows directly from its three thematic pillars.

**No hidden control flow.** An interrupt handler does not magically allocate stack frames or unwind—the calling convention you specify is exactly what the hardware will use. No hidden runtime trampoline adjusts the stack; if you choose `callconv(.Naked)`, you are responsible for saving and restoring every register. This prevents surprises when a tick handler runs with a partial stack frame.

**Explicit is better than implicit.** There is no built‑in concept of a “heap” or “scheduler.” You bring your own allocator (often `FixedBufferAllocator`) and explicitly pass it to any function that needs memory. The entry point, panic handler, and log backend are all explicitly wired via `std_options` or direct `@export`. This makes the boot sequence auditable line by line.

**Comptime over runtime magic.** Register maps, peripheral base addresses, clock configurations, and even entire interrupt vector tables can be generated at compile time from SVD or CMSIS headers. This eliminates runtime parsing, reduces binary size, and catches misconfigured clocks before the code ever reaches silicon. The same `comptime` machinery that builds generic data structures in hosted Zig builds register accessors in freestanding Zig—no need for a separate code generator.

The deliberate choice to avoid a separate `#![no_std]` ecosystem means that libraries written for desktop Zig can often be reused on a microcontroller with minimal changes—provided they accept an allocator and do not depend on OS features. The `std` library itself respects this: `std.log` can be redirected to a UART by setting a few `std_options` fields, and `std.testing` can run on embedded hardware with a custom panic handler that blinks an LED.

